do $do$
declare p jsonb := $pl$[{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-111-v2","t":"Carbohydrates","s":"A textile scientist investigates whether cellulose gives starch-coated cotton yarn resistance to breaking. The scientist has a solution that selectively removes cellulose but leaves starch unchanged. Which procedure would most directly test cellulose’s structural contribution?","a":"D","c":[["A","Divide yarn pieces of identical length and thickness from one batch randomly between the cellulose-removing solution and a matching solution lacking the removing agent, then measure the starch remaining on each piece.",false,"Energy-only misconception: Measuring an energy-storage carbohydrate does not assess the yarn’s structural strength. Starch remaining after treatment cannot establish whether cellulose helps the yarn resist breaking. Fix: Select a measurement that directly tests resistance to breaking."],["B","Place thin yarn pieces in the cellulose-removing solution and thick pieces of the same length in a matching solution lacking the removing agent, then measure the force needed to break each piece.",false,"Confounded-variable trap: Yarn thickness changes together with treatment and can influence breaking force. Any observed difference could reflect thickness rather than cellulose removal. Fix: Match yarn thickness as well as length between experimental groups."],["C","Place every yarn piece of identical length and thickness from one batch in the cellulose-removing solution, then measure the force needed to break each piece and report the mean.",false,"Missing-control trap: The mean describes treated yarn but provides no untreated comparison for identifying a treatment effect. Testing multiple pieces does not supply that comparison. Fix: Add matched yarn pieces receiving the same solution without the cellulose-removing agent."],["D","Divide yarn pieces of identical length and thickness from one batch randomly between the cellulose-removing solution and a matching solution lacking the removing agent, then measure the force needed to break each piece.",true,"Cellulose is a structural polysaccharide, so breaking force measures the function being investigated. Matching yarn dimensions and comparing treated yarn with an otherwise equivalent control isolates cellulose removal as the experimental difference."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-111-v3","t":"Carbohydrates","s":"A museum conservator asks whether cellulose helps starch-coated repair paper resist puncturing. A solution can remove cellulose while leaving the starch intact. Which procedure would best isolate cellulose’s contribution to the paper’s strength?","a":"B","c":[["A","Randomly divide equally thick pieces from one batch between the cellulose-removing solution and a matching solution without the removal agent, then measure the starch remaining in each piece.",false,"Energy-only misconception: Starch content measures an energy-storage carbohydrate rather than resistance to puncturing. This outcome cannot establish whether cellulose contributes to mechanical strength. Fix: Choose a force measurement that directly tests the proposed structural role."],["B","Randomly divide equally thick pieces from one batch between the cellulose-removing solution and a matching solution without the removal agent, then measure the force needed to puncture each piece.",true,"Cellulose is a polysaccharide that can provide structural support, making puncture force a relevant outcome. Matching paper thickness and solution conditions allows the comparison to isolate the effect of cellulose removal."],["C","Expose thin pieces to the cellulose-removing solution and thicker pieces from the same batch to a matching solution without the removal agent, then measure puncture force.",false,"Confounded-variable trap: Both paper thickness and cellulose removal differ between groups, and thickness can affect puncture force. Any strength difference would therefore have more than one possible explanation. Fix: Match paper thickness before assigning pieces to the two solutions."],["D","Expose equally thick pieces from one batch to the cellulose-removing solution, then measure their puncture forces and report the mean.",false,"Missing-control trap: The mean describes treated paper but provides no baseline for determining the treatment’s effect. Testing multiple treated pieces does not supply the missing comparison. Fix: Add matched paper pieces receiving the solution without the cellulose-removing agent."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-112","t":"Carbohydrates","s":"A plant seedling carries a mutation that inactivates the enzyme that joins glucose monomers into cellulose. Photosynthesis still produces glucose at normal rates, and the enzyme that joins glucose into starch works normally. Which prediction about the mutant seedling is best supported?","a":"C","c":[["A","It will be unable to store energy, because cellulose is the main energy-storage polysaccharide in plants.",false,"This confuses cellulose with starch. Cellulose builds plant cell walls for support, while starch stores energy, and starch synthesis is normal here.Fix: Before predicting, list each polysaccharide and its specific role: storage or structure."],["B","Its cell walls will stay strong, because starch is also a glucose polymer and will take over the structural role of cellulose.",false,"This assumes polymers built from the same monomer must share the same function. Starch and cellulose are both glucose polymers, but their different organization gives them different roles, and starch does not form cell walls.Fix: Ask whether the polymer's organization, not just its monomer, matches the function claimed."],["C","Its cell walls will be weak, so the seedling will have poor structural support, while its ability to store energy as starch will be largely unaffected.",true,"Cellulose is the structural glucose polymer of plant cell walls, so blocking its synthesis weakens support. Starch is a separate glucose polymer built by a different, working enzyme, so energy storage continues. Glucose itself is still made, so only the cellulose-dependent function is disrupted."],["D","It will lack glucose for cellular energy, because the mutation prevents glucose from being produced.",false,"This mistakes blocking a polymer's assembly for blocking its monomer's production. The stem states photosynthesis still makes glucose normally; only the joining of glucose into cellulose fails.Fix: Identify exactly which step the mutation blocks, then check which molecules upstream are still made."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-112-v1","t":"Carbohydrates","s":"Researchers maintain harvested potato tuber tissue with a steady supply of glucose. A treatment selectively prevents glucose monomers from being joined into starch, but glucose availability and cellulose assembly remain unchanged. Compared with untreated tissue, which outcome is best supported?","a":"A","c":[["A","The treated tissue will accumulate less starch for energy storage, while its cellulose-based structural support remains largely unaffected.",true,"Blocking starch assembly reduces the formation of an energy-storage polysaccharide. Cellulose provides structural support, and its assembly is unaffected, so the treatment does not directly disrupt that function."],["B","The treated tissue will lose structural support because starch is the principal polysaccharide that strengthens plant cell walls.",false,"Cellulose–starch role confusion assigns the structural function to the storage polymer. Cellulose supports plant cell walls, whereas starch stores energy; cellulose assembly continues in this tissue. Fix: Label each named polysaccharide as storage or structural before predicting the treatment's effects."],["C","The treated tissue will maintain normal carbohydrate energy storage because cellulose can replace starch wherever glucose polymers are needed.",false,"The same-monomer, same-function misconception treats glucose polymers as interchangeable. Their organization gives them different functions, so cellulose does not replace starch as the tissue's energy-storage polysaccharide. Fix: Check whether the replacement polymer has the required function, not merely the same monomer."],["D","The treated tissue will lack glucose for immediate energy use because blocking starch assembly prevents glucose from being produced.",false,"Polymer-assembly versus monomer-production confusion mistakes joining glucose units for making glucose. The treatment blocks starch formation, while the stem explicitly preserves glucose availability. Fix: Separate monomer availability from polymer assembly, then identify which process the treatment changes."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-112-v2","t":"Carbohydrates","s":"Wheat leaves normally accumulate starch during daylight. Researchers develop a wheat line in which glucose monomers cannot be joined into starch. Measurements show that glucose production and the assembly of cellulose remain normal. Compared with ordinary wheat, which change is best predicted in these leaves?","a":"B","c":[["A","Their structural support will decrease, because starch is the main polysaccharide supporting plant cell walls.",false,"Storage–structure confusion assigns cellulose’s role to starch. Starch stores energy, whereas cellulose supports plant cell walls; cellulose assembly remains normal in these leaves. Fix: Label each affected polysaccharide as storage or structural before predicting the consequence."],["B","Their capacity to store energy as starch will decrease, while cellulose-based structural support will remain largely unchanged.",true,"Starch stores energy in a polymer of glucose monomers, so preventing its assembly reduces starch storage. Cellulose provides structural support, and its assembly is unaffected. The disruption therefore separates an energy-storage function from a structural function."],["C","Their carbohydrate energy storage will remain unchanged, because cellulose can replace starch whenever glucose monomers are available.",false,"The same-monomer, same-function error treats glucose polymers as interchangeable. Their organization gives starch and cellulose different functions, so normal cellulose assembly does not replace lost starch storage. Fix: Check whether the proposed replacement polymer has the required function, not merely the same monomer."],["D","Their supply of glucose for immediate energy use will disappear, because preventing starch assembly also prevents glucose production.",false,"The monomer-production error confuses making glucose with assembling it into a polymer. The measurements establish that glucose production continues despite the failure of starch assembly. Fix: Separate monomer production from polymer assembly, then locate the disrupted step."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-112-v3","t":"Carbohydrates","s":"A herbicide is applied to the growing root tips of young corn plants. It blocks the enzyme that links glucose monomers into cellulose. Glucose from the leaves still reaches the root cells at normal levels, and the enzyme that links glucose into starch in those cells is unaffected. Which prediction about the treated root cells is best supported?","a":"B","c":[["A","The root cells will lose their stored energy reserves, because cellulose is the polysaccharide plants use to store glucose.",false,"This confuses cellulose with starch. Cellulose provides structural support in cell walls, while starch is the plant's energy-storage polysaccharide, and starch assembly is normal here. Fix: Before predicting, label each polysaccharide in the scenario as storage or structure."],["B","Newly built cell walls in the root tips will be structurally weak, while the root cells will continue to build starch reserves.",true,"Cellulose is the glucose polymer that gives plant cell walls their strength, so blocking its assembly weakens new walls. Starch is a separate glucose polymer made by an enzyme that still works, and glucose is still supplied, so energy storage continues."],["C","The root cell walls will remain sturdy, because starch is built from the same glucose monomers and can replace cellulose in the wall.",false,"This assumes polymers sharing a monomer must share a function. Starch and cellulose are both glucose polymers, but their different organization gives them different roles, and starch does not build cell walls. Fix: Ask whether the polymer's organization, not only its monomer, fits the function being claimed."],["D","The root cells will be starved of glucose for energy, because the herbicide prevents glucose from being made.",false,"This mistakes blocking a polymer's assembly for blocking production of its monomer. The stem states glucose still reaches the root cells normally; only the linking of glucose into cellulose fails. Fix: Identify the exact step the treatment blocks, then check which molecules before that step remain available."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-113","t":"Lipids","s":"Two fats have fatty acid chains of similar lengths. Fat X contains more unsaturated fatty acids and is liquid at room temperature, while fat Y contains more saturated fatty acids and is solid. Which explanation best accounts for this difference?","a":"D","c":[["A","Unsaturated fatty acids in fat X contain only single carbon–carbon bonds, which introduce kinks.",false,"Saturation reversal: saturated, not unsaturated, fatty acids contain only single carbon–carbon bonds. The kinks associated with unsaturation result from double bonds. Fix: Match saturated with single bonds and unsaturated with at least one double bond."],["B","Double bonds straighten fat X’s fatty acid chains, allowing tighter packing.",false,"Packing reversal: the double bonds associated with unsaturation introduce kinks rather than straighten the chains. Reduced tight packing helps explain why fat X is liquid. Fix: Trace the relationship from double bonds to kinks to reduced packing."],["C","Double bonds make fat X’s fatty acid chains hydrophilic, causing it to be liquid.",false,"Polarity confusion: carbon–carbon double bonds do not make fatty acid chains hydrophilic; the chains remain largely nonpolar. The relevant difference is chain packing, not attraction to water. Fix: Distinguish a fatty acid chain’s saturation from its polarity."],["D","Double bonds introduce kinks in fat X’s fatty acid chains, preventing tight packing.",true,"Unsaturated fatty acids contain carbon–carbon double bonds that introduce kinks. These kinks reduce tight packing, making fat X more likely to be liquid at room temperature."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-113-v1","t":"Lipids","s":"A bakery tests a modified canola oil in which some carbon–carbon double bonds in the fatty acid chains have been converted to single bonds. The chain lengths remain unchanged. Which prediction best connects this modification to the oil’s texture at room temperature?","a":"A","c":[["A","The oil tends to become firmer because fewer kinks allow the fatty acid chains to pack more tightly.",true,"Converting double bonds to single bonds increases saturation and reduces the kinks associated with unsaturation. Straighter chains pack more tightly, favoring a firmer texture at room temperature."],["B","The oil tends to become firmer because the chains become more unsaturated, and unsaturated chains contain only single carbon–carbon bonds.",false,"Saturation reversal: chains with only single carbon–carbon bonds are saturated, whereas unsaturated chains have at least one double bond. This modification increases saturation rather than unsaturation. Fix: Identify whether the modification increases or decreases the number of double bonds."],["C","The oil tends to become softer because removing double bonds introduces kinks that prevent tight packing.",false,"Packing reversal: removing the double bonds associated with kinks reduces bending rather than adding it. The resulting chains can pack more tightly, favoring firmness rather than softness. Fix: Follow the sequence from fewer double bonds to fewer kinks to tighter packing."],["D","The oil tends to become firmer because converting double bonds to single bonds changes the chains from hydrophilic to hydrophobic.",false,"Polarity confusion: both saturated and unsaturated fatty acid chains are largely nonpolar. The texture change follows altered chain packing, not a switch from attraction to water to avoidance of water. Fix: Check whether the proposed explanation concerns chain shape or attraction to water."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-113-v2","t":"Lipids","s":"A cosmetics manufacturer needs a fat that flows easily at room temperature for use in a skin lotion. The available fats have fatty acid chains of comparable lengths but differ in saturation. Which recommendation best explains how to obtain the desired consistency?","a":"C","c":[["A","Select a fat rich in unsaturated fatty acids because these chains have only single carbon–carbon bonds, which create bends.",false,"Saturation reversal: chains with only single carbon–carbon bonds are saturated, whereas unsaturated chains contain double bonds. The bends associated with unsaturation arise from double bonds, not single bonds. Fix: Check which bond type defines each saturation category before predicting texture."],["B","Select a fat rich in unsaturated fatty acids because double bonds straighten the chains and promote close packing.",false,"Packing reversal: double bonds associated with unsaturation produce bends rather than straighter chains. Close packing would favor a firmer fat, not the flowing consistency needed here. Fix: Link chain bending to packing before deciding whether a fat will flow."],["C","Select a fat rich in unsaturated fatty acids because double bonds bend the chains and reduce tight packing.",true,"Kinks produced by carbon–carbon double bonds keep unsaturated fatty acid chains from packing closely together. A fat with more of these chains is therefore more likely to remain liquid at room temperature."],["D","Select a fat rich in unsaturated fatty acids because double bonds make the chains hydrophilic and therefore liquid.",false,"Polarity confusion: adding carbon–carbon double bonds does not make fatty acid chains hydrophilic; they remain largely nonpolar. Their effect on consistency comes from altered packing, not increased attraction to water. Fix: Separate changes in chain shape from changes in attraction to water."]]},{"k":"APBIO-MCQ-SV-113-v3","t":"Lipids","s":"An agricultural laboratory examines oils extracted from sunflower seeds grown in two fields. The northern field’s oil contains a higher proportion of unsaturated fatty acids than the southern field’s oil, while fatty acid chain lengths are similar. Which prediction about the northern field’s oil at room temperature is best supported by its fatty acid structure?","a":"D","c":[["A","It should be more likely to remain liquid because unsaturated fatty acids have only single carbon–carbon bonds that create kinks.",false,"Saturation reversal: this explanation assigns single bonds to unsaturated fatty acids. Saturated fatty acids have only single carbon–carbon bonds; the kinks associated with unsaturation arise from double bonds. Fix: Identify whether each fatty acid has only single bonds or includes double bonds."],["B","It should be more likely to become solid because double bonds straighten fatty acid chains and promote tight packing.",false,"Packing reversal: this prediction treats double bonds as chain-straightening features. The kinks associated with unsaturation reduce tight packing rather than promote it. Fix: Link increased unsaturation to chain shape, then use that shape to predict packing."],["C","It should be more likely to remain liquid because double bonds make fatty acid chains hydrophilic.",false,"Polarity confusion: this explanation mistakes unsaturation for increased attraction to water. Fatty acid chains remain largely nonpolar; their packing explains the predicted consistency difference. Fix: Separate the question of chain polarity from the question of chain packing."],["D","It should be more likely to remain liquid because double bonds create kinks that reduce tight packing of fatty acid chains.",true,"A higher proportion of unsaturated fatty acids means more chains with carbon–carbon double bonds and associated kinks. These chains pack less tightly, favoring a liquid consistency at room temperature."]]}]$pl$::jsonb; r jsonb; c jsonb; v_ci uuid; v_civ uuid; n int;
begin
  if jsonb_array_length(p) <> 10 then raise exception 'chunk size'; end if;
  if exists (select 1 from app.content_items ci where ci.exam_pack_version_id='2d88ba5e-a6a3-43b8-bfae-9e5505a178a7' and ci.content_key in (select x->>'k' from jsonb_array_elements(p) x))
    then raise exception 'chunk already loaded'; end if;
  for r in select * from jsonb_array_elements(p) loop
    insert into app.content_items (exam_pack_version_id, content_key, item_type, title, status)
    values ('2d88ba5e-a6a3-43b8-bfae-9e5505a178a7', r->>'k', 'mcq', r->>'t', 'draft') returning id into v_ci;
    insert into app.content_item_versions (content_item_id, version_num, stem, stimulus, content_hash, status, review_status, canonical_answer_1)
    values (v_ci, 1, r->>'s', null, md5(r->>'k'), 'draft', 'tutor_review_pending', r->>'a') returning id into v_civ;
    for c in select * from jsonb_array_elements(r->'c') loop
      insert into app.mcq_choices (content_item_version_id, choice_key, choice_text, is_correct, rationale)
      values (v_civ, c->>0, c->>1, (c->>2)::boolean, c->>3);
    end loop;
  end loop;
  select count(*) into n from app.content_items ci join app.content_item_versions v on v.content_item_id=ci.id join app.mcq_choices m on m.content_item_version_id=v.id
   where ci.exam_pack_version_id='2d88ba5e-a6a3-43b8-bfae-9e5505a178a7' and ci.status='draft' and ci.content_key in (select x->>'k' from jsonb_array_elements(p) x);
  if n <> 40 then raise exception 'chunk 5: expected 40 choice rows, got %', n; end if;
  raise notice 'chunk 5: loaded 10 draft items';
end
$do$;
